  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...A powerful coastal storm will continue to pull away \nfrom the region through Saturday with improving conditions. \n\nStrong low pressure continues to slowly slide northward away from \nthe region. Weak high pressure will very briefly settle in to the \nregion tonight through Saturday.  Widespread clouds will continue to \nbe present through tonight. Dry conditions and decreasing \nclouds are expected on Saturday. Temperatures will be near to \nslightly above normal through Saturday. It will remain breezy \nhowever, with winds gusting as high as 35 mph this afternoon, \nand in the 20-25 mph range tonight and Saturday. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Tidal flooding will linger overnight in the back bays of \nOcean County, NJ and Sussex County, DE.\n\nThe remaining Coastal Flood Warnings have been cancelled as \nconditions improve for most areas. However, Coastal Flood\nAdvisories have been issued for Sussex County, Delaware and \nOcean County, NJ through 4 AM Saturday. Water levels are\ndecreasing in the backbays but another round of minor tidal\nflooding is likely overnight. Water levels should continue to\ndecrease following this high tide as winds remain offshore and\nallow water to drain.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...Widespread rain and windy conditions is expected \nacross our region with the next cold front late Sunday into Monday. \n\nA new area of low pressure will be lifting north and west of the \nregion Sunday night through Monday. This is a very different track \nthan the track that yesterday's system took. The main impacts we are \nwatching for this system are widespread rain particularly Sunday \nNight, and windy conditions on Monday.\n\nFor the heavy rain concern, most model guidance shows rain amounts \nin the 0.5 to 1 inch range, which is low enough to limit any flash \nflood threat at this point. In addition, the timing of the rain \novernight, should limit instability, thus decreasing the risk for \nlocalized downpours.\n\nThe strongest winds are going to come behind a cold front during the \nday on Monday. There is less agreement between the models on wind \nspeeds (as compared to rain amounts), but at this point, it looks as \nif peak gusts of up to 45 mph will be possible over portions of the \nregion. If this trend continues, a wind advisory may be needed \nparticularly for the higher terrain of the southern Poconos and NW \nNJ, and along the coastal areas.",
